var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"#StatusWindows","page":"Home","text":"Documentation for StatusWindows.\n\n","title":"StatusWindows"},{"category":"module","location":"#StatusWindows.StatusWindows","page":"Home","text":"StatusWindows\n\nBorderless, always-on-top desktop panels you fill with your own content — a conky-like window, driven from Julia.\n\nA panel is a GLFW window with no decorations. Each tick the whole panel is drawn into a Cairo image surface and blitted to the screen as a single texture, so the drawing API is plain Cairo and the GPU work is one quad.\n\nusing StatusWindows\n\np = Panel(width = 300, height = 220, x = 40, y = 40)\n\ndraw!(p) do c\n    heading!(c, \"SYSTEM\")\n    kv!(c, \"host\", gethostname())\n    bar!(c, \"cpu\", 0.42)\nend\n\nrun!(p, refresh = 1.0)\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.StatusWindows"},{"category":"constant","location":"#StatusWindows.BODY_FONTS","page":"Home","text":"Monospaced body fonts worth using, best first, one list per platform.\n\nThere is no font every system has, so naming a single default would leave two platforms out of three rendering in whatever fontconfig felt like substituting. Each list starts with the face that ships with that system: DejaVu on Linux, Menlo on macOS (present since 10.6), Consolas on Windows.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.BODY_FONTS"},{"category":"constant","location":"#StatusWindows.MATH_FONTS","page":"Home","text":"Math fonts worth using, best first. Whichever is installed wins; if none are, poormansmath! falls back to the panel's body font, which still renders Greek and the common operators.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.MATH_FONTS"},{"category":"type","location":"#StatusWindows.Canvas","page":"Home","text":"Canvas\n\nWhat your draw function receives. Wraps a Cairo context plus the panel's Style, and carries a vertical cursor so widgets stack down the panel without you tracking coordinates.\n\nFields w and h are the panel's logical size in pixels; x and y are the current cursor. Reach for c.cr when you want to drive Cairo directly.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.Canvas"},{"category":"type","location":"#StatusWindows.Panel","page":"Home","text":"Panel(; kwargs...)\n\nAn undecorated, always-on-top window you fill with your own drawing.\n\nKeyword arguments:\n\nwidth, height — panel size in logical pixels.\nx, y — position of the top-left corner on the desktop, in the same logical pixels.\nscale — how many device pixels go to a logical one. :auto asks the window system, which is what makes the panel come out the same physical size on a 96 dpi monitor and on a 240 dpi one; pass a number to pin it.\ntransparent — request a transparent framebuffer, so the panel blends with the wallpaper. Needs a compositor; see the README for caveats.\nontop — keep the panel above other windows.\ndecorated — draw a title bar. Off by default; turn it on while debugging placement.\nmovable — drag the panel with the left mouse button.\npassthrough — let clicks fall through to whatever is underneath. Implies the panel cannot be dragged.\nstyle — a Style controlling colours, font and spacing.\nforce_x11 — on Linux, force GLFW's X11 backend. GLFW's Wayland backend cannot position its own windows (the compositor decides, and SetWindowPos is a silent no-op), so this is what makes x and y mean anything under a Wayland session. Ignored if DISPLAY is unset.\n\nGive it content with draw!, then start it with run!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.Panel"},{"category":"type","location":"#StatusWindows.Style","page":"Home","text":"Style(; kwargs...)\n\nAppearance of a Panel: colours, font and spacing. Colours are (r, g, b, a) tuples with components in 0..1.\n\nThe defaults are a dark translucent panel with light text. bg's alpha is what makes the panel see-through, so drop it to 0.0 for the classic conky look where only the text floats over the wallpaper.\n\nfont and mathfont are empty by default, which means \"pick the best installed one\" — see fontof and mathfontof.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.Style"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.advance-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"Advance of str in the current font, i.e. how wide it will draw.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.advance"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.ascent-Tuple{Canvas}","page":"Home","text":"Font ascent, so a widget can turn a cursor position into a text baseline.\n\nCairo.jl wraps text_extents but not font_extents, so go to the C library directly. cairo_font_extents_t is five doubles: ascent, descent, height, maxxadvance, maxyadvance.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.ascent"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.at!-Tuple{Canvas, Real, Real}","page":"Home","text":"at!(c::Canvas, x, y)\n\nMove the drawing cursor. Both coordinates are in logical pixels from the panel's top-left corner.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.at!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.bar!-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString, Real}","page":"Home","text":"bar!(c::Canvas, label, frac; color, height, showpct)\n\nA labelled progress bar. frac is clamped to 0..1.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.bar!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.blit-Tuple{UInt32, UInt32, UInt32, Integer, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"Draw the texture over the whole framebuffer.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.blit"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.content_width-Tuple{Canvas}","page":"Home","text":"Width available between the left and right margins.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.content_width"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.draw!-Tuple{Function, Panel}","page":"Home","text":"draw!(f, p::Panel)\n\nRegister f as the panel's content. It is called on every tick with a Canvas, and whatever it draws becomes the panel.\n\ndraw!(p) do c\n    heading!(c, \"SYSTEM\")\n    kv!(c, \"load\", string(Sys.loadavg()[1]))\nend\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.draw!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.ensure_surface!-Tuple{Panel}","page":"Home","text":"Reallocate the Cairo surface when the framebuffer size changes.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.ensure_surface!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.fontfamilies-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"fontfamilies() -> Vector{String}\n\nEvery font family Pango can actually use on this machine, sorted.\n\nThis is the list that matters for the body font and for poormansmath!: families are resolved by name through fontconfig, and a name that is not on this list gets silently substituted rather than raising. Cached after the first call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.fontfamilies"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.fontof-Tuple{Style}","page":"Home","text":"fontof(st::Style) -> String\n\nFamily the text widgets should draw with.\n\nAn empty st.font means \"the best installed entry of BODY_FONTS\", which is what lets one Style look right on Linux, macOS and Windows without naming a font that only one of them ships.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.fontof"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.heading!-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"heading!(c::Canvas, str)\n\nA section title in the accent colour, underlined by a rule.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.heading!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.hrule!-Tuple{Canvas}","page":"Home","text":"hrule!(c::Canvas; color = c.style.dim)\n\nA hairline across the panel's content width.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.hrule!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.kv!-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString, Any}","page":"Home","text":"kv!(c::Canvas, key, value; keycolor, valuecolor)\n\nA label on the left and a value flushed right — the workhorse row for a status panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.kv!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.math!-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"math!(c::Canvas, tex; color, size, align)\n\nDraw real TeX math — fractions, radicals, limits stacked over big operators — and advance the cursor.\n\nThis needs MathTeXEngine.jl, which is a weak dependency: the method only exists once you load it.\n\nusing StatusWindows, MathTeXEngine   # both, in either order\n\ndraw!(p) do c\n    math!(c, raw\"\\frac{\\alpha}{\\beta} + \\sqrt{x^2}\")\nend\n\nThis is a genuine TeX layout engine, and it carries its own fonts, so it renders identically on every machine whether or not any math font is installed. It is heavier than poormansmath!: glyphs are rasterized and composited one at a time, which is fine at the once-a-second refresh a panel runs at.\n\nReach for poormansmath! when you want plain sub/superscripts in the panel's own font and would rather not take the dependency.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.math!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.mathfontof-Tuple{Style}","page":"Home","text":"mathfontof(st::Style) -> String\n\nFamily poormansmath! should draw with.\n\nAn empty st.mathfont means \"pick the best installed math font, and fall back to the body font if there is none\" — so a machine with STIX gets STIX and a bare one still works.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.mathfontof"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.mathwidth-Tuple","page":"Home","text":"mathwidth(c::Canvas, tex; size)\n\nWidth in pixels that math! would need for tex. Like math!, this comes from the MathTeXEngine extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.mathwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.move!-Tuple{Panel, Integer, Integer}","page":"Home","text":"Move the panel's top-left corner to (x, y), in logical pixels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.move!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.paint_background!-Tuple{Cairo.CairoContext, Style, Real, Real}","page":"Home","text":"Wipe the surface to fully transparent, then lay down the panel background.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.paint_background!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.poormansmath!-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"poormansmath!(c::Canvas, tex; color, size, align)\n\nDraw a line of mathematical notation with no dependency beyond Cairo, and advance the cursor.\n\ntex is LaTeX-ish: _ and ^ become real subscripts and superscripts, and Cairo's token table maps a few hundred commands (\\alpha, \\sum, \\leq, \\infty, ...) to their Unicode characters. Braces group, so x_{i+1}^{2n} works.\n\npoormansmath!(c, \"\\sigma^2 = 0.37\")\npoormansmath!(c, \"\\sum_{i=1}^n x_i \\leq \\infty\")\n\nThis is Pango markup underneath, not a TeX engine, and the name is a warning: there is no fraction, radical, matrix or limits-over-the-operator layout, and the result depends on which fonts the machine happens to have. math! does the job properly; this is what you get for free.\n\nCommands outside the table are passed through verbatim rather than raising, so \\hat\\beta draws a literal \\hat followed by β. Check anything unusual against Cairo.tex2pango before trusting it.\n\nText is not escaped, so a literal <, > or & in tex will confuse Pango — write \\lt, \\gt and \\& instead.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.poormansmath!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.poormansmathwidth-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"poormansmathwidth(c::Canvas, tex; size = c.style.size)\n\nWidth in pixels that poormansmath! would need for tex.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.poormansmathwidth"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.printstyle-Tuple{}","page":"Home","text":"printstyle(; kwargs...) -> Style\n\nA Style for paper: white ground, dark ink, square corners.\n\nThe on-screen default is a dark translucent panel designed to sit on a wallpaper; on paper that is an ink-hungry black rectangle, and the transparency flattens to white anyway. Keyword arguments override individual fields as usual.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.printstyle"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.refresh!-Tuple{Panel}","page":"Home","text":"refresh!(p::Panel)\n\nRedraw the panel once, immediately. run! calls this on a timer; call it yourself if you drive the loop.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.refresh!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.render-Tuple{Any, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"render(content, path; width, height, style) -> path\nrender(p::Panel, path; width, height, style) -> path\n\nDraw to a file. The format comes from the extension: .pdf, .svg, .eps/.ps give vector output with selectable text, .png gives a bitmap.\n\nrender(p, \"panel.pdf\")                       # a live panel's content\nrender(\"panel.svg\"; width = 300) do c        # or any draw function\n    heading!(c, \"report\")\n    kv!(c, \"n\", 1024)\nend\n\nVector surfaces measure in points (1/72 inch), so a 300×220 render is about 4.2×3.1 inches on paper. Defaults to printstyle rather than the panel's own dark palette; pass style to override.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.render"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.run!-Tuple{Panel}","page":"Home","text":"run!(p::Panel; refresh = 1.0)\n\nBlock, redrawing every refresh seconds until the panel is closed or stop! is called. Escape closes the panel.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.run!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.setcolor!-Tuple{Cairo.CairoContext, NTuple{4, Float64}}","page":"Home","text":"Apply an RGBA tuple to the context.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.setcolor!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.setfont!-Tuple{Canvas}","page":"Home","text":"Select the style's font at size (defaults to the style's own size).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.setfont!"},{"category":"function","location":"#StatusWindows.spacer!","page":"Home","text":"spacer!(c::Canvas, px = c.style.line / 2)\n\nLeave px pixels of vertical space.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.spacer!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.sparkline!-Tuple{Canvas, Any}","page":"Home","text":"sparkline!(c::Canvas, values; height, color, lo, hi)\n\nA filled line chart of values across the panel's content width. lo and hi fix the vertical scale; by default it tracks the data.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.sparkline!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.start!-Tuple{Panel}","page":"Home","text":"start!(p::Panel; refresh = 1.0)\n\nLike run! but on a task, so the REPL stays usable. Stop it with stop!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.start!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.stop!-Tuple{Panel}","page":"Home","text":"Ask the panel's loop to finish; the window closes on the next tick.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.stop!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.text!-Tuple{Canvas, AbstractString}","page":"Home","text":"text!(c::Canvas, str; color, size, bold, align)\n\nDraw one line of text and advance the cursor. align is :left, :right or :center.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.text!"},{"category":"method","location":"#StatusWindows.upload!-Tuple{UInt32, Matrix{UInt32}}","page":"Home","text":"upload!(tex, buf)\n\nPush the Cairo backing store into tex. buf is a (width, height) column-major Matrix{UInt32}, which in memory is exactly the row-major ARGB32 image Cairo drew. On a little-endian machine those words are laid down as B,G,R,A bytes, which is what GL_BGRA + GL_UNSIGNED_INT_8_8_8_8_REV expects.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"StatusWindows.upload!"}]
}
